Homework 6: Median Combination
October 2026

I used the FITS image organization and data cubes created during
Practicum 4 as the starting point for Homework 6.

My main Homework 6 Python file is:

hw6_prakash.py

The input data directory is:

hw6_data/

The object cube has shape:

(9, 1024, 1024)

The dark cube has shape:

(2, 1024, 1024)


Problem 2a
----------
I median-combined the stack of dark images using the NumPy median
function:

meddark = np.median(darkcube, axis=0)

The important option is axis=0. The first axis of darkcube represents
the individual dark exposures, so taking the median along axis 0
calculates the median value at every corresponding y,x pixel location.

The 3D dark cube therefore becomes one 2D median-combined dark image.

No loop was required for this operation.

The median-combined dark image has shape:

(1024, 1024)


Problem 2b
----------
I printed the requested pixel from the median-combined dark image.

The value of pixel index [217, 184] was:

19.0


Problem 2c
----------
I added a HISTORY entry to the dark FITS header stating that the image
is a median-combined dark frame.

The command used was:

darkhead.add_history("Median-combined dark frame.")


Problem 2d
----------
I wrote the median-combined dark image, together with the modified FITS
header, to:

dark_13s_med.fits

I used astropy.io.fits.writeto() to write the file.


Problem 2e
----------
I first saved the value of pixel index [217, 184] in the first object
frame before dark subtraction.

The original value was:

10879.0

I then subtracted the median-combined dark image from every object image
using:

objcube -= meddark

NumPy broadcasting applies the 2D median-dark frame to every image in
the 3D object cube. This allows the subtraction to be done without a
loop.

The use of -= also performs the operation in place instead of creating
an unnecessary additional object cube.

After dark subtraction, pixel index [217, 184] in the first object
frame was:

10860.0

The difference is:

10879.0 - 10860.0 = 19.0

This agrees with the median-dark value of 19.0 at the same pixel,
showing that the dark subtraction behaved as expected.

I added a HISTORY entry to the first object's FITS header stating that
the median-combined 13-second dark frame had been subtracted.

I then wrote only the first dark-subtracted object image to the filename
specified in the homework:

hw7_prakash_prob2_graph1.fits

The two FITS files produced by the homework were therefore:

dark_13s_med.fits
hw7_prakash_prob2_graph1.fits

